      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. To adjust the tension of your serpentine belt, you don't typically need a special tool. Here's a simple way to check and adjust the belt tension:\n\n1. Locate the tensioner pulley: This is usually a small, round pulley that holds the serpentine belt in place. It's often found near the front of the engine.\n\n2. Check the belt tension: Grasp the belt with both hands and pull it away from the pulleys. If the belt is too loose, it will sag and may not return to its original position. If it's too tight, it will be difficult to pull away and may feel like it's being pulled by a strong goblin.\n\n3. Adjust the tensioner: If the belt is too loose, you can usually adjust the tension by turning the tensioner pulley. This is often done by turning a small screw or knob on the tensioner itself. Turn it clockwise to increase tension or counterclockwise to decrease it. Be careful not to over-tighten, as this can cause the belt to wear out prematurely.\n\n4. Check the belt again: After adjusting the tension, check the belt again to ensure it's neither too loose nor too tight. The belt should have a slight give when pressed",
